1
00:06:33,750 --> 00:06:39,750
Dungeons & Dragons: Wrath of the Dragon God is a action,
adventure, fantasy film directed by Gerry Lively.

2
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:13,500
Due to a curse from his former master Profion, Damodar survived his death by Ridley Freeborn as an undead entity in pursuit of an evil artifact for some
hundred years, so that he might be capable of unleashing unstoppable destruction on Izmir and the descendants of those who caused his demise.

3
00:19:41,250 --> 00:19:47,250
Damodar, played by Bruce Payne.

4
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:21,000
Berek, played by Mark Dymond.

5
00:32:48,750 --> 00:32:54,750
Melora, played by Clemency Burton-Hill.

6
00:39:22,500 --> 00:39:28,500
Lux, played by Ellie Chidzey.

7
00:45:56,250 --> 00:46:02,250
Nim, played by Tim Stern.

8
00:52:30,000 --> 00:52:36,000
Dorian, played by Steven Elder.

9
00:59:03,750 --> 00:59:09,750
Dungeons & Dragons: Wrath of the Dragon God is a 2005 direct-to-video American fantasy adventure
film directed by Gerry Lively and written by Brian Rudnick and Robert Kimmel.

10
01:05:37,500 --> 01:05:43,500
The second instalment in the Dungeons & Dragons film series, it serves as a stand-alone sequel to
Dungeons & Dragons (2000), which in turn was based on role-playing game of the same name.

11
01:12:11,250 --> 01:12:17,250
The only returning actor is Bruce Payne, reprising his role as Damodar.

12
01:18:45,000 --> 01:18:51,000
The film premiered at the Sci-Fi Channel on October 10, 2005.

13
01:25:18,750 --> 01:25:24,750
It was released in theaters in Europe as well as some parts of North America
and Latin America, and released on DVD on February 7, 2006.

14
01:31:52,500 --> 01:31:58,500
Dungeons & Dragons: Wrath of the Dragon God was followed by Dungeons & Dragons 3: The Book of
Vile Darkness, released direct-to-DVD in the United Kingdom on August 9, 2012.

15
01:38:26,250 --> 01:38:32,250
The film explores themes of fighter, royalty, curse, dragon, d&d.
